  "image_name": "81d20addefabf01ee1a5627c36efbed1.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a conceptual map of light–matter phenomena in a 2D material across both time and length scales. At the center, a metallic near-field tip excites a localized optical response labeled polaritons, shown as a confined red-blue hotspot on an atomically thin lattice. Blue and red wave-like arrows indicate how excitation and energy flow connect ultrafast optical processes to different quasiparticles and energy ranges.\n\nOne diagonal axis summarizes timescales, progressing from steady-state or continuous-wave behavior through picoseconds and femtoseconds, with thermalization, optical transitions, and electron–phonon coupling highlighted as key steps. A second diagonal axis shows the energy hierarchy, from low-energy phonons and vibrations in the meV range up to plasmons and excitons at eV energies.\n\nThe bottom axis expands the relevant spatial scales, from about 10 nm to 1 µm. Along this range, the image links nanoscale edge polaritons and molecular aggregates to larger moiré lattices, nanodevices, plasmonic structures, domains, domain boundaries, and even cells. Overall, the figure conveys that nano-optical probing can bridge ultrafast dynamics, broad energy scales, and structures spanning nanometers to micrometers in complex materials and devices.",
